2. [MAJOR] src/repl/render.rs:245 — `.table` of a list whose records hold no keys (`[{}]`, reachable: the parser accepts an empty record, src/parser.rs:2717-2757) prints two blank lines and says nothing — why it matters: the empty *list* is named ("The list is empty: no rows, and no keys to name.", :232) but the "rows with no keys" case renders an empty table, so the reader cannot tell a table from a failure; this is a new branch with no test on either side — concrete fix: name the case the way the empty list is named when `keys` is empty (e.g. "the records hold no keys: there is nothing to name") and add `edge_a_table_of_records_with_no_keys_says_so` (unit + integration).
3. [MAJOR] tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs:254 — the history audit's rule ("every unreviewed commit in HEAD's history is named with a reason") was replaced for two subjects by a prefix waiver, and phase-063 added *its own* phase's prefix to that list — why it matters: `is_delivery_subject` (:836) makes any commit whose subject starts with "resume phase-063:" acknowledged by construction — never unreviewed, never stale — so the commit carrying this phase's unreviewed code is forgiven by a rule this phase wrote; and DELIVERY_REASON (:266) describes an earlier round's contents, omitting the new direct dependency (`unicode-width`, Cargo.toml:40 + Cargo.lock:774) and the audit file's own change, so the record of *what* unreviewed code is in the tree is now wrong by omission — the exact failure the audit was written to prevent. It is also scope: a REPL-printing phase rewriting the gate that judges its own commit. Concrete fix: keep the subject rule but make the acknowledgement record what the commit actually carries (measure the delivery commit's numstat at audit time and write it into the reason), and record phase-063's prefix as a named deviation in the audit's own docs. I could not check the pipeline's commit-creation behaviour, so I cannot say whether the SHA problem is as described — that, and a run of `git log --format=%H%x09%s HEAD`, is what would settle it.
4. [MINOR] src/repl/render.rs:197 — `.pretty` and `.json` write the characters a terminal acts on raw, where `.table` escapes them — why it matters: `one_line`/`mangles_the_terminal` (:350-385) escape bidi marks, zero-width marks and the C1 controls, but the other two renderers write their leaves through `json_stringify` → `json_escape_text` (src/runtime.rs:1735-1757), which escapes only characters below U+0020; so `.json "\u202eabc"` (the lexer supports `\uXXXX`, src/lexer.rs:326) puts a raw right-to-left override on the terminal and reverses the line, while `.table` of the same value spells it `\u{202e}` — the same value mangles the terminal through two of the three new commands — concrete fix: escape the bidi/zero-width marks in `write_pretty` (which is not tied to `json.stringify`'s document), and either escape them in `.json` or say in SPEC.md/REPORT.md that `.json` is a document first and a terminal rendering second.
5. [MINOR] src/repl/render.rs:93 — the phase's central invariant (".json is the document the language's own `json.stringify` writes") is not pinned by any test — why it matters: `write_json` re-implements the list and record structure by hand and shares only the *leaf* writer with `runtime::json_stringify` (src/runtime.rs:1796), so a change to the language writer's shape (`, ` → `,`) silently makes `.json` differ from `json.stringify` while every test still passes; the REPORT asserts the identity as an invariant but nothing checks it — concrete fix: a test that evaluates `json.stringify` on the same value inside a session and asserts the two documents are byte-identical.
6. [MINOR] src/repl/render.rs:1276 — the round-3 width assertion `cell_width(glyph) == 2` for the skin-tone sequence `\u{1f44d}\u{1f3fb}` depends on how unicode-width 0.2.2 measures an emoji modifier sequence (base 2 + modifier 2 = 4 if it is a plain sum) — why it matters: if that is 4, `edge_a_table_column_holding_an_emoji_glyph_is_padded_by_the_glyph_it_is_drawn_in` is red for the same reason as finding 1, and the whole round-3 premise ("an emoji that is one glyph is padded by that one glyph") does not hold — concrete fix: settle it with the same run that settles finding 1; if the width is 4, the test and the REPORT's round-3 claim must change. I could not check this without executing the suite, so I am flagging it as unverified rather than asserting it.

